WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
So, I went from $300,000

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.760
in student loan debt to making seven

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.240
figures on YouTube in 3 years. No

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.840
inheritance, no connections, no trust

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.920
fund, just a gray bedsheet, a mediocre

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.720
camera, and a system that I figured out

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.960
the hard way. And I know that a lot of

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.840
people are stuck in a similar situation

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.600
to the one that I was in, and I know

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.200
that a lot of people want to start on

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.360
YouTube. So, today I'm going to be

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.040
breaking down every single turning point

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.000
and breaking down the most important

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.440
lessons that I learned so that you don't

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.120
have to make the same mistakes that I

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.600
did, and you can learn the lessons

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.520
faster. Okay, so let me take you back to

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.680
where this all started. I'm living in

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.320
Bellingham, Washington, just outside of

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.800
Seattle. I'm a freshly graduated

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.520
pharmacist making around $10,000 a

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.480
month, and on paper that sounds great,

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.200
right? But here's what the paper doesn't

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.440
show you. I had $300,000

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.800
in student loan debt, and I had six

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.280
roommates. Yes, six. And I was stuck

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.120
inside of a pharmacy box for minimum

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.520
12-hour shifts where I couldn't even

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.519
step outside to take a breath of fresh

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.160
air. No, like literally I could not step

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.880
outside. That was against the rules. And

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.520
I'm not going to lie, for the first few

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.480
months I was actually happy because I

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:16.000
grew up poor, like Section 8 housing

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.480
poor, trailer park poor, even homeless

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.720
at one point poor. So, going from that

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.360
to making $10,000 a month meant that I

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.080
could actually buy stuff. I could go to

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.560
restaurants. I didn't have to tell my

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.440
friends that I couldn't afford to come

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.360
out with them anymore. But that initial

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.600
high faded fast. Then the pandemic hit

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.360
and everything got worse. Most of my

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.880
staff quit, some were scared of getting

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.040
sick, others wanted to collect

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.880
unemployment and get paid to do nothing,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.480
and I was doing three to five people's

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.360
jobs by myself. And one day the air

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.520
conditioner broke, so I was literally

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.440
sweating, running around the pharmacy

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.240
trying to get everybody's prescriptions

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.320
ready. Oh, and we also got robbed three

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.720
times in a 6-month period because

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.560
everyone was wearing masks, so you

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.480
couldn't even identify who was doing it.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.680
And so, on top of being super stressed

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.000
and overworked, hating my job, I was

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.880
also in fear of my physical safety. And

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.479
then one day, I checked my student loan

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:09.800
balance, and it was over $300,000.

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:12.840
I spent $300,000 so that I could stand

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.280
inside of a box 12-plus hours a day, do

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.720
five people's jobs, and also be in fear

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.560
of my physical safety. But honestly, I'm

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.600
pretty mentally strong, and through all

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.520
of that, I just powered through. But

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.440
there was one thing that was actually my

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.160
breaking point. One of my coworkers had

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.040
been working for the company that I was

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:32.560
working for for 40 years. And there was

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.800
a day where she actually hit her 40th

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.640
year with the company. And on that day,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.040
I happened to be working. And the

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.280
company did absolutely nothing for her.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.440
Zero recognition whatsoever. The only

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.200
thing they did is they sent out an

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.760
automated letter to her, basically

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.760
saying she could select some sort of

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.080
gift from a registry that's probably

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.160
worth about $5 or less, and she would

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.480
get that gift shipped to her for free.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.720
That's it. Nothing else. The manager and

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.120
I actually ended up buying her a cake,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.959
and if we hadn't done it ourselves, she

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.120
would have gotten zero recognition. So,

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:05.360
think about this for a moment. She spent

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.720
40 years of her life working for a

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.360
company. Basically dedicated her entire

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.640
life to the company. And when she hit

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.840
her 40th year, she got zero recognition.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.280
She was literally just a cog in a

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.040
machine. And that was my breaking point.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.959
That's when I knew that something had to

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.760
change. And so, I started looking for a

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.200
way out. I started typing into YouTube

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.000
and Google how to make money online or

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.480
what side hustles make money, and all

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.600
kinds of different stuff like that. And

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.120
like most people, I fell for the flashy

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.239
marketing, the Lamborghinis, the I make

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.400
50K a month from my laptop on the beach

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:39.120
stuff, and I bought courses from people

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.760
like Kevin David, who I'm pretty sure

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.280
was just copying other people's courses

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.720
and then reselling them. I bought Tai

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.160
Lopez's SMMA course. I tried social

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.440
media marketing, and I even got a few

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.440
clients, but nothing was sustainable.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.800
And then there was selling physical

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.080
products like Amazon FBA and Shopify.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.920
So, here's one of the products that I

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.760
had. It was called the Booty Blaster,

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.480
and it was basically this workout band

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.520
thing that you strap to your leg, and it

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.520
was supposed to work your glutes. And I

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.720
lost a bunch of money on that one, and

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.320
I'm not even going to show you what the

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.160
product looked like because it's just

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:09.040
embarrassing. But, here's the thing.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.200
Every single one of those failures

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.959
taught me something. The business models

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.040
all worked. My mindset was the thing

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:18.079
that was wrong. And then I found Sam

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.079
Ovens' Consulting Accelerator course.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:22.240
And what I liked about this guy is he

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:24.640
didn't promise easy. He basically said,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.360
"Becoming a successful entrepreneur is

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.240
going to be painful. You're going to

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.200
need to improve yourself as a human

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.960
being first." And that hit me

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.600
differently than anything else that I'd

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:35.600
heard because everyone else was

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.880
basically saying that I deserve to make

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.560
money, and making money was super easy,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.400
and basically just telling me what I

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.520
wanted to hear. But, Sam basically said

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.400
that I shouldn't be trying to make

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.960
money. I should be trying to become a

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.080
person who deserves to make money. And

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.919
that was the mindset shift that changed

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.800
everything. Now, right around this time,

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:54.480
a friend of one of my roommates invited

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.480
me to this rich guy's house. And this

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.520
guy had multiple luxury cars in his

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.080
driveway. Beautiful house, three dogs

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.360
that are different colors, and I'm not

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.480
even trying to make this up. They were

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.080
named Obama, Trump, and Hillary. And

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.720
basically, they were a husband and wife

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:08.840
content creator team, and they were

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.760
targeting businesses with software, and

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.760
they were making serious money. Like,

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.919
$10,000 in a couple of hours type of

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.520
money. And at the end of the night, he

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:18.200
was gracious enough to hang out with me

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.080
even though I was a nobody, and he

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.480
pointed at a camera, and he said this,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.200
"If there's one thing you take away from

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.240
tonight, know this. This camera will

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.360
make you rich. Don't forget that." And I

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.080
took that to heart. I went home, I set

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.640
up a gray bedsheet behind my desk as a

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.960
background. If you watch my early

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.200
videos, that's what you're seeing, a

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.640
literal bedsheet. And I put books on my

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.520
chair to raise it up so the camera

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.520
wouldn't show my bed in the frame, and I

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.400
started recording. And around the same

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.280
time, I met a guy named Sam. He'd moved

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.240
to the US from Lebanon, and he couldn't

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.520
get a normal job because of immigration

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.480
stuff. So, he was forced to start a

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.000
business and he was making a good six

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.840
figures a year teaching photography on

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.320
YouTube. And he took me under his wing,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.080
taught me the ropes, and that's when

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.560
things started clicking. And my first

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:02.080
banger video was called the most useless

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:03.960
degrees. It got over 4 million views and

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.880
I started getting traction fast. This is

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.920
when I hired my first virtual assistant

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.160
Paulo, who's now my general manager by

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.080
the way. He basically handles

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.960
thumbnails, editing, scripts, emails,

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:15.560
comments, everything. And within months

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.000
I was making $10,000 a month on YouTube.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.240
Then it was 20,000, then it was 30,000,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.520
and I was already making two to three

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.919
times more than what I made as a

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.120
pharmacist. But I was afraid to quit my

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:27.000
job at that point. I had spent so much

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.320
time going to school, spending all those

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.960
years getting my doctorate and taking

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.360
out a massive amount of student loans,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.280
and I was afraid to quit. Even though I

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.919
was making way more money from my side

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.480
hustle than my job and I was spending

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:40.000
way less time on my side hustle, I was

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.760
still afraid to quit. I was thinking

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.840
that maybe it's just a temporary thing,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.600
and maybe people are just, you know,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.800
watching YouTube more because it's the

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.440
pandemic. All these excuses popped into

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.000
my head. And because of this, I got

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.840
stuck around $30,000 a month for a long

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.720
time. I simply just did not have the

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.080
bandwidth to scale the business beyond

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.160
that. But eventually I saved a little

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.240
bit of money up and I took a big leap

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.640
and I decided to actually quit my job.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.800
And I'll tell you that was one of the

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.640
scariest and best decisions that I've

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.440
ever made. And the thing that actually

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.600
was the turning point that made me quit

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.640
my job was when I actually took a trip

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.600
to Puerto Rico with my best friend. And

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.320
we actually stayed in a hostel and I met

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.880
people that were in this hostel. They

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.680
were actually working remotely making

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.000
really good money while they were

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.280
traveling. And they actually told me how

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.760
much their expenses were and it really

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.480
wasn't all that much. In fact, I was

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:27.040
paying way more money to live in

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.480
Bellingham, Washington than they were to

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.040
travel the world. And to be honest, I

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.080
was very risk-averse, but that really

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:33.919
appealed to me. I could travel the world

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.520
and spend way less money than what I was

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:37.280
actually spending living in a place that

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.080
I didn't really want to live. So that is

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.280
when I decided to quit my job and I was

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:42.680
making 10 to 30k per month from YouTube

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.680
and I started traveling the world,

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:46.120
right? Puerto Rico was first, but I also

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.960
spent a lot of time in the San Juan

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.040
Islands. I went up to Canada. I traveled

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.040
to Thailand, the Philippines, et cetera,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.320
right? I went to the Philippines, for

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.520
instance, and visited Polo. And life was

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:57.400
good, but I hit a wall. You see, I met

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.040
other YouTubers who had way smaller

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.480
channels than me, and they were making

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.440
way more money. And I'm sitting there

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.840
like, "What the heck am I doing wrong?"

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.640
And here's what I figured out. I was

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.640
thinking like a content creator, not

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.920
like a business owner. I was making most

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.320
of my money from AdSense, which are

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.920
basically the ads that pop up on your

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.040
channel. So, I read this book called

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:15.800
Expert Secrets by Russell Brunson, and

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:17.760
it completely changed how I thought

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.160
about monetization. I started layering

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.160
in affiliate marketing more, digital

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.600
products, coaching, sales funnels. And

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:25.919
my income went from 30K a month to 100K

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.000
a month. Same channel, same audience,

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:29.800
same number of videos, just completely

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.000
different monetization. And that's when

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.520
I realized that subscriber count means

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.520
absolutely nothing when it comes to

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.479
revenue. So, now I'm making 100K per

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.479
month with the same amount of views that

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.320
I was making 10 to 30K per month with

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:43.000
before. Sounds like a dream, right?

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.600
Well, I was actually kind of running

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.839
around like a chicken with my head cut

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:48.880
off. I was doing everything. Scripts,

00:08:46.839 --> 00:08:51.040
ideas, editing feedback, emails,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.560
managing a team of 10 to 15 people. It

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.440
was a mess. I was making great money,

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:57.839
but I was burning out. Then I read this

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.160
book called Traction by Gino Wickman.

00:08:57.839 --> 00:09:01.560
And this is the book that took me from

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.040
100K per month to 300K per month and

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.960
beyond, while reducing my work to about

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.800
4 hours a week. I implemented a system

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:09.520
called EOS, or the entrepreneurial

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.160
operating system. And I gave everyone my

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.160
team clear roles, clear

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.040
responsibilities, and clear metrics, and

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:17.040
I started removing myself from every

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.920
process that didn't require me. And all

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.600
I had to do was show up for one general

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.560
meeting each week, and then record

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.000
videos, and that's it. I didn't have to

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.480
write the scripts, I didn't have to come

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.760
up with the ideas, I didn't have to do

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.160
the thumbnails, I didn't have to do the

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.960
editing. It was all automated for me.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.560
And here's the thing that blows people's

